    "question": {
      ":question/id": "85519727-c081-5269-a196-6e8814b500c0",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Given $f(x,y)=\\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $\\nabla f=\\mathbf 0$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A gradient vanishes exactly when every component vanishes. Differentiate with respect to each variable, holding the others constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}&=2 x - 2 \\\\ f_{y}&=2 y + 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet all components equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 x - 2&=0 \\\\ 2 y + 4&=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo write the simultaneous equations compactly, let $u=x - 1,\\ v=y + 2$. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=0 \\\\ v&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Recover the original coordinates using $x=u + 1,\\ y=v - 2$.\n\nTherefore, the complete set of points in the domain is $(1,-2)$. Each satisfies every component equation.",
